The change flips the sign-prefix check in `parseYamlInteger$1` (used only by `intJsonTag`, i.e. `JSON_SCHEMA`) from `||` to `&&`. Since `value[0]` can never be both `"-"` and `"+"`, the sign is never stripped anymore. For plain decimals this is harmless (`parseInt("-123", 10)` still yields -123), but for explicitly tagged signed binary/octal/hex forms — which `intJsonTag`'s explicit pattern accepts — the value now falls through to `parseInt(value, 10)`, which stops at the radix prefix: `!!int "-0x1F"` yields `-0` instead of `-31`, and `!!int "+0x10"` yields `0` instead of `16`.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "test/core/proof.test.mjs", "content": "import { describe, it } from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert';
import { load, dump, JSON_SCHEMA } from 'js-yaml';

describe('proof', () => {
  it('fails on the patched build', () => {
    // The JSON-schema int resolver must strip a leading sign before decoding
    // the 0x/0b/0o prefix. The patch turns that check into `&&`, which is
    // never true, so signed radix forms reach parseInt(value, 10) and the
    // digits after the prefix are silently dropped (yielding 0 or -0).
    assert.strictEqual(load('!!int "-0x1F"', { schema: JSON_SCHEMA }), -31);
    assert.strictEqual(load('!!int "+0x10"', { schema: JSON_SCHEMA }), 16);
    // Control: plain signed decimals still resolve identically.
    assert.strictEqual(load('!!int "-41"', { schema: JSON_SCHEMA }), -41);
  });
});
"}, "note": "The `&&` condition in parseYamlInteger$1 (the intJsonTag/JSON_SCHEMA resolver) is always false, so a leading sign is never stripped; explicitly tagged signed non-decimal integers like `!!int "-0x1F"` or `!!int "+0x10"` are then parsed with parseInt(value, 10), returning -0/0 instead of -31/16."}
```

